    "subject": "Every inline Code and HTML node's position, compared with cmark-gfm's own --sourcepos over the CommonMark spec fixture.",
    "purpose": "Forty rows, and every one of them is a row where THIS SIDE IS RIGHT. It read zero for the whole of Stage 0a: every inline position agreed with cmark-gfm's, including the ones that were wrong, which is exactly what specs/positions/places.json's closing note says this oracle cannot see.\n\n  34  code-span-covers-its-backticks   A code span's scope covers its own delimiters, as emphasis covers its asterisks, a link its brackets and parens, and strikethrough both tilde pairs. Upstream reports the extent of the CONTENT instead, which is a defect this repository inherited from it: `` `foo` `` on a line of its own put the span at columns 2..4 of a five-byte line, and `` `` `` alone on a line put it at column 3 of a two-byte line, which is not a place at all. A scope exists so a consumer can map a node back to the source it came from, and the source a code span came from includes the ticks that make it one. ANSWERED BY THE OWNER at 8.4 (Q45), after the alternative had been built and measured twice.\n   6  raw-html-loses-its-closing-byte   `a <b` / `c> d` gives a raw-HTML node whose literal ends at 2:2 and whose scope ended at 2:1, so its closing `>` belonged to no node at all. places.json names this input, pins it as a golden row in regression.txt, and assigns it to Step 8. Step 8.1's projection closed it. Upstream still reports 2:1 on all six.\n\nAGREEING WITH AN AUTHORITY THAT IS ITSELF WRONG IS NOT AGREEMENT. This ledger's value was always that it would go non-zero the moment this engine's inline positions stopped being cmark-gfm's; what it did not say until Step 8 is that every such moment so far would be an improvement. A row here is a DISAGREEMENT, not a defect, and each one's closedBy says which side is right.",
